          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 10 Membrane Structure"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.328,
            0.507,
            0.737
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE LIPID BILAYER 604  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Glycerophospholipids, Sphingolipids, and Sterols Are the Major Lipids in Cell Membranes 605  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Phospholipids Spontaneously Form Bilayers 606  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Lipid Bilayer Is a Two-dimensional Fluid 608  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Fluidity of a Lipid Bilayer Depends on Its Composition 609  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Despite Their Fluidity, Lipid Bilayers Can Form Domains of Different Compositions 610  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Lipid Droplets Are Surrounded by a Phospholipid Monolayer 611  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Asymmetry of the Lipid Bilayer Is Functionally Important 612  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Glycolipids Are Found on the Surface of All Eukaryotic Plasma Membranes 613  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 614  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "MEMBRANE PROTEINS 615  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Membrane Proteins Can Be Associated with the Lipid Bilayer in Various Ways 615  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Lipid Anchors Control the Membrane Localization of Some Signaling Proteins 616  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "In Most Transmembrane Proteins, the Polypeptide Chain Crosses the Lipid Bilayer in an "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\alpha"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "-Helical Conformation 617  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transmembrane "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\alpha"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Helices Often Interact with One Another 619  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Some "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\beta"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Barrels Form Large Channels 619  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Membrane Proteins Are Glycosylated 621  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Membrane Proteins Can Be Solubilized and Purified in Detergents 622  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Bacteriorhodopsin Is a Light-driven Proton "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "(\\mathrm{H}^{+})"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Pump That Traverses the Lipid Bilayer as Seven "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\alpha"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Helices 625  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Membrane Proteins Often Function as Large Complexes 627  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Membrane Proteins Diffuse in the Plane of the Membrane 627  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cells Can Confine Proteins and Lipids to Specific Domains Within a Membrane 629  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Cortical Cytoskeleton Gives Membranes Mechanical Strength and Restricts Membrane Protein Diffusion 630  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Membrane-bending Proteins Deform Bilayers 632  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 633  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 634  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 635"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.748,
            0.505,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 11 Small-Molecule Transport and Electrical Properties of Membranes 637"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.778,
            0.506,
            0.932
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "PRINCIPLES OF MEMBRANE TRANSPORT 637  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protein-free Lipid Bilayers Are Impermeable to Ions 638  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Two Main Classes of Membrane Transport Proteins: Transporters and Channels 638  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Active Transport Is Mediated by Transporters Coupled to an Energy Source 639  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 640  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "TRANSPORTERS AND ACTIVE MEMBRANE TRANSPORT 640  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Active Transport Can Be Driven by Ion-Concentration Gradients 642  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transporters in the Plasma Membrane Regulate Cytosolic pH 644  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "An Asymmetric Distribution of Transporters in Epithelial Cells Underlies the Transcellular Transport of Solutes 645  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Three Classes of ATP-driven Pumps 646"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.525,
            0.098,
            0.924,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A P-type ATPase Pumps "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " into the Sarcoplasmic Reticulum in Muscle Cells 647  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Plasma Membrane "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{Na^{+}-K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Pump Establishes "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{Na^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " and "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Gradients Across the Plasma Membrane 648  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "ABC Transporters Constitute the Largest Family of Membrane Transport Proteins 649  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 651  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CHANNELS AND THE ELECTRICAL PROPERTIES OF MEMBRANES 651  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Aquaporins Are Permeable to Water but Impermeable to Ions 652  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Ion Channels Are Ion-selective and Fluctuate Between Open and Closed States 653  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Membrane Potential in Animal Cells Depends Mainly on "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Leak Channels and the "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Gradient Across the Plasma Membrane 655  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Resting Potential Decays Only Slowly When the "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{Na^{+}-K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Pump Is Stopped 655  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Three-dimensional Structure of a Bacterial "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Channel Shows How an Ion Channel Can Work 657  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mechanosensitive Channels Allow Cells to Sense Their Physical Environment 659  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Function of a Neuron Depends on Its Elongated Structure 661  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Voltage-gated Cation Channels Generate Action Potentials in Electrically Excitable Cells 662  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Myelination Increases the Speed and Efficiency of Action Potential Propagation in Nerve Cells 666  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Patch-Clamp Recording Indicates That Individual Ion Channels Open in an All-or-Nothing Fashion 666  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Voltage-gated Cation Channels Are Evolutionarily and Structurally Related 668  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Different Neuron Types Display Characteristic Stable Firing Properties 668  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transmitter-gated Ion Channels Convert Chemical Signals into Electrical Ones at Chemical Synapses 669  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chemical Synapses Can Be Excitatory or Inhibitory 670  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Acetylcholine Receptors at the Neuromuscular Junction Are Excitatory Transmitter-gated Cation Channels 671  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Neurons Contain Many Types of Transmitter-gated Channels 672  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Many Psychoactive Drugs Act at Synapses 673  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Neuromuscular Transmission Involves the Sequential Activation of Five Different Sets of Ion Channels 673  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Single Neurons Are Complex Computation Devices 674  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Neuronal Computation Requires a Combination of at Least Three Kinds of "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{K^{+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Channels 675  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Long-term Potentiation in the Mammalian Hippocampus Depends on "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{Ca^{2+}}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Entry Through NMDA-Receptor Channels 677  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Use of Channelrhodopsins Has Revolutionized the Study of Neural Circuits 678  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 679  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 680  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 681"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.528,
            0.694,
            0.922,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 12 Intracellular Organization and Protein Sorting 683"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.526,
            0.726,
            0.923,
            0.932
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE COMPARTMENTALIZATION OF CELLS 683  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "All Eukaryotic Cells Have the Same Basic Set of Membrane-enclosed Organelles 683  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Evolutionary Origins Explain the Topological Relationships of Organelles 686  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Macromolecules Can Be Segregated Without a Surrounding Membrane 688  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Multivalent Interactions Mediate Formation of Biomolecular Condensates 690  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Biomolecular Condensates Create Biochemical Factories 690  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Biomolecular Condensates Form and Disassemble in Response to Need 693  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proteins Can Move Between Compartments in Different Ways 694  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Sorting Signals and Sorting Receptors Direct Proteins to the Correct Cell Address 695  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Construction of Most Organelles Requires Information in the Organelle Itself 697  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 697"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
